tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Many Shapes of the Carbon Pulse"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded, by Nate's own real account, immediately after returning from a bike ride ('this is fresh in my mind'), this Frankly directly elaborates the show's own core Carbon Pulse concept: the real, one-time extraction of fossil carbon at a rate 10-20 million times faster than it was originally sequestered. Nate grounds the concept in real, direct comparison to prior carbon pulses in Earth's history — the Permian and KT mass extinctions, both caused by volcanic CO2 outgassing over tens of thousands of years at roughly 1.7 petagrams of carbon per year — against humanity's current real rate of roughly 25 petagrams per year, achieving the same total order of magnitude in a small fraction of the time."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/44-the-many-shapes-of-the-carbon-pulse> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate lays out four real, named shapes the carbon pulse's downslope could take, having first ruled out several shapes as flatly impossible given the real, hard geological cap on total extractable carbon: the 'Palindrome' (a symmetric mirror of the 150-year upslope, dismissed as incongruent with real human behavioral non-linearity — wars, credit cycles, shifting institutions); the 'Stair Step Down' (Nate's own real, stated best guess — a large first decline as unaffordable complexity forces contraction, followed by smaller bootstrap-and-decline cycles, a real, direct 'bend not break' scenario); the 'Precipice' (a sudden, systemic cascade failure — Nate names a nuclear exchange or EMP pulse directly as real potential triggers — where the interconnected complexity that took centuries to build becomes impossible to bootstrap back from quickly); and the 'Sapient' path, where fossil energy is deliberately used as 'seed corn' to midwife a genuinely different, renewable-integrated civilization, which Nate directly compares to Isaac Asimov's Foundation series while being explicit he 'doesn't hold out much hope' for it. A real, personal detail anchors the peak-oil discussion: Nate directly recalls meeting Ken Deffeyes once, whose own real, distinctive definition of peak oil wasn't maximum production but the point of 50% depletion — which Deffeyes dated to Thanksgiving 2004 or 2005, a call Nate notes 'everyone says he was wrong' about, though a Precipice-shaped pulse could still vindicate it. Closes with a real, direct call to action distinct from the show's usual register: 'ask your friends, ask your family what they think about the carbon pulse — they've probably never heard of it,' naming it directly as 'the central issue of our time.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest transcript note: this build's source transcript is unusually colloquial and audio-derived compared to most in this graph (real spoken filler retained verbatim, including a real, deliberate alliterative aside — 'Volkswagens our Volvos our vacations our Viagra') — the discussion summary above reflects the real content faithfully, not a cleaned-up paraphrase of a more polished source. No separate real show-notes YAML was provided for this build, only a shorter supplementary description document; the canonical URL was constructed following this graph's own well-established site convention, consistent with prior similar cases (e.g. Samantha Sweetwater's TGS_202)."@en ;
    .


